{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773084280,"nonce":"f3iSleA7clZeLttxoI3XAvaVx1g=","title":"Brainstorm: specialized visual representation for node/edge types in knowledge graphs","message":"Currently HumbleB treats all nodes and edges uniformly in its rendering — entities are generic circles, predicates are unlabeled edges, and the visual language makes no distinction between e.g. a person, a concept, a document, or a claim.\n\nAs we think about future use cases (particularly operational graphs like agent activity, issue tracking, branch topology), it may be worth exploring whether the KG can have a richer, more semantically grounded visual language:\n- Node shapes or icons keyed to entity type (person vs concept vs agent vs document)\n- Edge styles keyed to predicate type (e.g. dashed for uncertain, solid for asserted, animated for live relationships)\n- Color coding for credence bands\n- Cluster/group visual treatment for entity neighborhoods\n\nThis is speculative brainstorming. The goal is not to prescribe a specific design but to open the question of whether the knowledge graph can be made more readable and domain-specific without sacrificing its epistemic generality. This may also inform future integration with a Mandible operational graph view, where specialized visual representation is a core requirement.","files":null}]}